{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789558491,"nonce":"ZXcpV5dehuRSsLEx8J2IBujMGIc=","message":"Audit after a1ffebe: LLVM cross-tier outgoing calls already use FunctionAbi and pass aggregate arguments by pointer (llvm_backend.rs compile_cross_tier_call). The incoming LLVM function declaration still translates each HIR aggregate parameter to an LLVM struct value (declare_function), and compile_function maps HIR parameter values from those by-value LLVM parameters. This is the specific mismatch that prevents promoting advance. An entry wrapper that merely loads the pointer into a struct value is insufficient for mutable List headers: a callee may reallocate and must update the caller-visible header. The incoming ABI and all aggregate-value uses in the LLVM body need to agree on pointer semantics, followed by mutation and aliasing tests before removing the guard.","files":null}]}